// 02 · handlers — effects declare, handlers decide, resume is typed
//
// An effect says what a function MIGHT do — read state, write a line, fork a
// thread. A handler says what those operations MEAN at the point you install
// it. The same source runs two ways under two handlers; the code that
// performs an effect never names the handler that catches it. That is the
// whole idea, and it is why the `~>` chain is a capability stack: the
// outermost handler is the least trusted boundary, the innermost the most
// empowered.
//
// The load-bearing detail is `resume`. A handler arm can resume the computation
// that performed the effect — zero times (it aborts), once (ordinary control),
// or many times (backtracking, search, the medium's own oracle). You never
// declare which; Mentl INFERS the cardinality from the arm's body. An arm that
// calls resume once under no loop is OneShot and compiles to a direct call; an
// arm that resumes under recursion is MultiShot and captures a real
// continuation. Write the body that means what you want; the medium proves the
// cardinality.
//
// Run it:   mentl run  lib/tutorial/02-handlers.mn   ← prints 3 (three incs, then read)
// Swap the handler in main to counter_logged and run again — same source, a
// second reality, each tick narrated.
import io

// Counter — a two-op effect. `inc` bumps the count; `read` returns it.
effect Counter {
  inc()
  read() -> Int
}

// Handler one: keep the count in handler state, do nothing else. Each arm
// resumes exactly once with no surrounding loop, so both ops infer OneShot.
handler counter_in_memory with count = 0 {
  inc() => resume() with count = count + 1,
  read() => resume(count),
}

// Handler two: the SAME effect, a different surface. Every `inc` also prints a
// line. `triple_inc` below does not change; only the chain decides which arm a
// performed `inc` reaches.
handler counter_logged with count = 0 {
  inc() => {
    "tick " ++ int_to_str(count) ++ "\n" |> print_string
    resume() with count = count + 1
  },
  read() => resume(count),
}

// User code. It performs against Counter and never mentions a handler; `with
// Counter` in the signature is the medium proving the effect is present.
fn triple_inc() with Counter = {
  inc()
  inc()
  inc()
  read()
}

// main installs the in-memory handler with `~>`. Change it to `~> counter_logged`
// to get the narrated reality — nothing else moves.
fn main() = {
  let total = triple_inc() ~> counter_in_memory
  total |> int_to_str |> print_string
}
